[Music]  what does the future of work look like  offices around the world are  reorganizing themselves  but not everyone wishes to return to  fixed workplaces once the pandemic  allows it  some have acquired a taste for working  from home  others still miss the physical divide  between the job and home life  and employers have mixed opinions too  many want to support allowing employees  the freedom to decide when and where  they work  for others this freedom is actually the  start of a free fall  into chaos  [Music]  welcome to your covet 19 special i'm  chris kober in berlin in march 2020 many  companies didn't have a choice the  raging coronavirus pandemic forced  governments to enact strict social  distancing rules overnight many offices  were shot and companies started a huge  remote working experiment  now that vaccine drives have been  underway at least in industrialized  nations and emerging economies how much  of remote working is here to stay  all he needs are a computer and an  internet connection  carlo almendral doesn't need an office  like many other filipinos he escaped the  lockdown in the capital manila to work  remotely on the beach  i didn't realize how  stressed out and anxious i was about the  pandemic until i got here and started  walking around on the beach with a mask  on and realized that like nobody else is  kind of doing it  [Music]  many have discovered the digital nomad  lifestyle  and it's hard for them to go back  some have let go of desk work completely  preferring a much more untethered model  welsh call center consultant jason  griffin wants to show the world that  work can be performed from literally  anywhere at all  provided there's enough battery power  it's a message he wants to share widely  the traditional five days in the office  monday friday nine to five they're gone  they're not here anymore people are  going to be split between home and  office if not home all the time and it's  exciting because  it fits people's life so much better  everything's going to be better  but the problem is not everyone may be  on board  the big problem that the major players  have is that they believe that the  best work can be done when the people  are bundled together in the one office  so to speak so there's going to be a  little bit of a tension there  and tensions can rise too when one is  forced to work from home and care for  children stuck there too  the yap family in taiwan knows all too  well having to concentrate on the job  and household chores while keeping  children engaged is a challenge  i'm so bored online learning is doing  all the things i did at school at home  i feel lazy because there's no one to  push me  but once it's safe to leave home will  offices ever be full again  in germany a minority can imagine  working exclusively from home  over 30 percent only want to work in the  office  but a significant majority prefers a mix  of office life and telecommuting  for now who gets which model will differ  from person to person and what their  bosses say  let's get more on this from sharon  parker she's the director of the center  for transformative work design at curtin  university's future of work institute in  perth australia welcome to dw sharon um  in the early days of the pandemic you  co-authored a research paper and in it  you outlined four key challenges to  people working remotely work home  interference ineffective communication  procrastination and loneliness have  these improved throughout the last  months  thanks chris yeah great question and i  guess the answer to that is they've  improved for some people but not  necessarily all people and that's i  guess one of the important things about  remote work during this time that people  have had very different experiences  and you know you know this from dinner  party conversations and so on if you're  someone who's at home in a small  apartment and you've got young children  and you know you haven't got your own  workspace and so on then those  challenges that you mentioned you know  can be really huge and daunting but for  other people who haven't had perhaps the  same situations  they've loved working from home and  thrived on it and actually you know are  talking about wanting to have more  working from home into the future so  people's experience have been very  diverse but absolutely some people have  experienced those challenges that you  mentioned  right i mean despite these diverse  experiences a microsoft report found  that almost 80 percent of those surveyed  said they would like to continue working  from home at least to some degree after  the pandemic  is that likely to happen  yeah and actually our own research  suggested very similar numbers uh of  people  um is it likely to happen uh actually  interestingly here in perth is a bit of  a test case because as i mentioned we've  been out of lockdown for about a year  we are definitely seeing more hybrid  models of work because i guess a few  things have happened  workers have discovered some of them  that this is something they want to  continue they've built new skills  they've become more confident  technology has advanced during this time  actually the technology is much much  better for this sort of conversation  that we're having right now and also  companies have learned that they can  trust people that they can still have  productivity and so um  they've also learned they could save  some office costs so there's been a lot  of forces coming together to suggest  that we we won't probably go back to how  it was before  exactly what it will look like  it's still uncertain but i think there's  going to be a lot more hybrid working  around into the future  now speaking of technology i mean there  is a large chunk of people  that say uh they feel overworked they  feel exhausted partly because of a  dramatic increase in digital  collaboration including emails meetings  remote meetings chats  groups working together on documents if  remote working is here to stay  what does a redesign look like  well we need to whether people are  remote working or whether people are  working in the office we need to give  attention to  these aspects of work that we know from  decades of research affect people  psychologically and you mentioned their  workload so one of the things we've seen  during covert when people are working  from home a lot even simple things like  the fact that people don't have to  commute  sometimes people spend that time just  working more you know and then you've  got some of those challenges of things  like um  constant zoom meetings and back-to-back  zoom meetings and it's very fatiguing  it's very tiring and research shows that  it's more tiring than those face-to-face  conversations  for all sorts of reasons  so you know what going into the future  we're going to have to pay serious  attention to that and say okay how do we  make sure that those workloads are  manageable that people are switching off  that we are giving people proper breaks  between meetings and so on and that's  this topic of work redesign and it's  it's been important for a long time in  in work in the office and it's going to  become a more important topic  for working from home into the future as  well let me ask you is all of this a  debate in very rich countries how much  working from home is possible in regions  where technological standards are much  lower  yeah you're absolutely right in a sense  we've got a privileged position um um  compared to  to some countries but also some jobs um  you know not all jobs can be done from  home as we know and we've talked a lot  about essential workers and and the  pressures that they've been under during  this covert um and i have to say you  know in the early days we we spent a lot  of time of of the pandemic saying well  actually we're lucky we we're sort of  talking about these struggles of remote  working but actually we've got a job you  know we've got a job that can be done at  home  um you know and we we've got health and  all those other things so sometimes you  do have to put these things in  perspective but i think the most  important point is that whether you work  from home whether you work in the office  there's things that you can do to make  work better and that's what we've got to  focus on you know teach our managers  educate our managers and others to  create work in which people um you know  have got reasonable workloads have got  some autonomy  you know and do those things that we  know really enable people to thrive and  flourish in their work  sharon parker of curtin university in  perth australia thank you for your  insights  whether we like it or not working from  home has kept many of us out of crowded  subways or buses during our commute and  away from potentially infectious office  meetings thereby lowering the risk of  catching the coronavirus  but we have to leave the house at some  point right and how protected are we  then here's one of your questions to our  science correspondent derek williams  how likely are to catch covid19 outside  for example from someone running by  for me one of the most striking aspects  of this pandemic is the amount of  research that's gone into trying to  answer  seemingly simple questions like this  which turn out to actually be  pretty incredibly complex i mean who  would have guessed even a year ago  that we'd have gotten so  obsessed with the highly abstract world  of aerosol distribution physics both  in enclosed rooms and outside back to  the question though we we now know that  while it certainly might be possible to  catch covet 19 outdoors  it's much much less likely than it is  indoors in poorly ventilated spaces  where people spend extended periods of  time that's because unlike outdoors  aerosols accumulate in enclosed spaces  and increase the chance  that you might inhale enough of of what  an infected person has exhaled  to catch the disease so  though that's an amount i'd like to note  by the way that we we still don't know  with any certainty  in general  the consensus seems to be that in  addition to the indoors outdoors aspect  of the equation  the other factors that play major roles  in infection are  how long exposure lasts  how close  an infected person comes to you  and the amount of time that you're  around them  the who only recommends masks in outdoor  settings  where physical distancing can't be  maintained  like crowds  and not for people doing intense  physical activity that's because pretty  much all the indicators point to the  conclusion  that the jogger panting by you in the  park  just doesn't really present that big of  a risk  [Music]  you

